# Python QA Automation Engine

![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.12%2B-3776AB?style=for-the-badge&logo=python&logoColor=white)
![Playwright](https://img.shields.io/badge/Playwright-1.61.0-2EAD33?style=for-the-badge&logo=playwright&logoColor=white)
![pytest](https://img.shields.io/badge/pytest-9.1.1-0A9EDC?style=for-the-badge&logo=pytest&logoColor=white)
![CI](https://img.shields.io/badge/CI-GitHub%20Actions-2088FF?style=for-the-badge&logo=github-actions&logoColor=white)

An end-to-end web UI automation framework built with **Python, Playwright, and pytest**.

The project demonstrates practical QA automation techniques by testing authentication and e-commerce checkout workflows against **SauceDemo**.

---

##  Project Overview

The **Python QA Automation Engine** is a UI test automation framework designed to provide reliable and repeatable automated testing for critical user workflows.

The current test suite focuses on two important areas:

- Authentication
- E-commerce checkout

The framework uses the **Page Object Model (POM)** to separate test logic from page interaction logic, making the tests easier to read, maintain, and extend.

The project also includes:

- Positive and negative test scenarios
- Data-driven testing
- Reusable pytest fixtures
- Page Object Model architecture
- Playwright browser automation
- HTML test reporting
- GitHub Actions continuous integration

**Application Under Test (AUT):**

[SauceDemo](https://www.saucedemo.com/)

---

#  Testing Objectives

The purpose of this project is to demonstrate how automated UI testing can be used to validate important application workflows.

The framework currently validates:

### Authentication

- Successful login with valid credentials
- Login failure with an incorrect password
- Login failure for a locked-out user
- Login failure with invalid credentials

### Shopping and Checkout

- Adding a product to the shopping cart
- Verifying the cart item count
- Verifying the selected product
- Navigating to checkout
- Entering customer information
- Completing an order
- Verifying the successful order confirmation

---

#  Test Coverage

| Area | Scenario | Type |
|---|---|---|
| Login | Successful login | Positive |
| Login | Incorrect password | Negative |
| Login | Locked-out user | Negative |
| Login | Invalid username | Negative |
| Shopping Cart | Add Sauce Labs Backpack | Functional |
| Shopping Cart | Verify cart item | Functional |
| Checkout | Enter customer information | End-to-End |
| Checkout | Complete order | End-to-End |
| Checkout | Verify order confirmation | End-to-End |

> The parameterized login test contains multiple data sets, allowing the same test logic to validate different invalid-login scenarios.

---

#  Tech Stack

| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Python 3.12+** | Programming language |
| **Playwright** | Browser automation |
| **pytest** | Test framework and test runner |
| **pytest-playwright** | Playwright integration with pytest |
| **pytest-html** | HTML test reporting |
| **python-dotenv** | Environment variable management |
| **GitHub Actions** | Continuous integration |
| **Page Object Model** | Test architecture/design pattern |

---

#  Framework Architecture

The project follows the **Page Object Model (POM)** design pattern.

Instead of placing selectors and browser interactions directly inside the test cases, page-specific actions are maintained inside dedicated page classes.

```text
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │       pytest        │
                 │    Test Runner      │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │    Test Scenarios   │
                 │                     │
                 │ test_login.py       │
                 │ test_checkout.py    │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │    Page Objects     │
                 │                     │
                 │ LoginPage           │
                 │ CheckoutPage        │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │      Playwright     │
                 │ Browser Automation  │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │      SauceDemo      │
                 │ Application Under   │
                 │       Test          │
                 └─────────────────────┘
```
#  Project Architecture

The project follows a **Page Object Model (POM)** structure, separating test scenarios from page-specific browser interactions.

```text
Python-QA-Automation-Engine/
│
├── .github/
│   └── workflows/
│       └── playwright.yml
│
├── pages/
│   ├── login_page.py
│   └── checkout_page.py
│
├── conftest.py
├── test_login.py
├── test_checkout.py
├── requirements.txt
├── .gitignore
└── README.md
```

### File and Folder Responsibilities

| File / Folder | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `.github/workflows/` | Contains the GitHub Actions CI workflow |
| `pages/` | Contains Page Object classes |
| `login_page.py` | Handles login page interactions |
| `checkout_page.py` | Handles checkout and shopping-cart interactions |
| `conftest.py` | Contains shared pytest fixtures |
| `test_login.py` | Contains authentication test scenarios |
| `test_checkout.py` | Contains the end-to-end checkout test |
| `requirements.txt` | Lists Python project dependencies |
| `.gitignore` | Specifies files and folders that Git should ignore |
| `README.md` | Project documentation |

# Test Execution Flow
**Login Test**

```text
Open SauceDemo
      ↓
Navigate to Login
      ↓
Enter Credentials
      ↓
Click Login
      ↓
Validate Result
```

**For negative scenarios:**
```text
Open SauceDemo
      ↓
Enter Invalid Credentials
      ↓
Click Login
      ↓
Capture Error Message
      ↓
Compare Against Expected Result
```

**Checkout Test**
```text
Login
  ↓
Open Products
  ↓
Add Sauce Labs Backpack
  ↓
Verify Cart Badge
  ↓
Open Cart
  ↓
Verify Product
  ↓
Start Checkout
  ↓
Enter Customer Information
  ↓
Continue
  ↓
Finish Order
  ↓
Verify "Thank you for your order!"
```
# Prerequisites
Before running the project, make sure you have:

- Python 3.12 or newer
- Git
- pip
-  Internet access for the SauceDemo application and Playwright browser installation


**Check your Python version**
```text
python --version
```
# Installation
**1. Clone the repository**
```
git clone https://github.com/Mihlee/Python-QA-Automation-Engine.git
```
**2. Create a virtual environment**

**Windows**
```
python -m venv venv
```
Activate it:
```
venv\Scripts\activate
```
**macOS / Linux**
```
python3 -m venv venv
```
Activate it:
```
source venv/bin/activate
```
```
When the environment is active, your terminal should display something similar to:  
(venv)
```
# Install Dependencies
Install all project dependencies: 
``` 
pip install -r requirements.txt
```
The project uses pinned versions of its main testing dependencies to provide a more consistent environment.

# Install Playwright Browsers

After installing the Python dependencies, install the Playwright browser:
```
playwright install chromium
```
The current CI workflow also installs Chromium for automated execution.

# Running the Tests

Make sure your virtual environment is activated before running the tests.

**Run the complete test suite**
```
pytest
```
**Run tests with detailed output**
```
pytest -v
```
**Run tests with the browser visible**     
For demonstrations and debugging, use:
```
pytest --headed -v
```
**Run Specific Tests**  

**Login tests**
```
pytest test_login.py -v
```
**Checkout test**
```
pytest test_checkout.py -v
```
# Generate an HTML Test Report

The project includes pytest-html for generating a test execution report.

**Run:**
```
pytest --html=report.html --self-contained-html
```
This creates:
```
report.html
```
Open the report in your browser to review the test results.  
The generated report is excluded from Git tracking through .gitignore.

#  Data-Driven Testing

The login test uses pytest.mark.parametrize to execute the same test logic with different sets of data.

Example scenarios include:

```
Username: standard_user
Password: wrong_password
Expected: Username and password do not match
```
```
Username: locked_out_user
Password: secret_sauce
Expected: User has been locked out
```
```
Username: invalid_user
Password: secret_sauce
Expected: Username and password do not match
```
# Reusable Fixtures

The project uses conftest.py to define reusable pytest fixtures.

The logged_in_page fixture:

1. Opens SauceDemo
2. Retrieves the configured credentials
3. Enters the credentials
4. Logs into the application
5. Provides the authenticated page to the test

This allows tests that require authentication to reuse the same setup instead of repeating the login steps.

